.TH TIMES 3C "August 1, 1992"
.UC 4
.SH NAME
times \- get process times
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B "#include <sys/times.h>
.PP
.B clock_t times(struct tms *\fIbuffer\fR);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.ft B
For non-POSIX applications this interface is obsoleted by getrusage(2).
.ft R
.PP
.I Times
returns time-accounting information
for the current process
and for the terminated child processes
of the current process.
All times are in 1/HZ seconds, where HZ is 60.
.PP
The 
.I tms
structure contains the following members:
.IP
.PP
.RS
.IP "\fBMember Type\fR" 20
\fBMember Name\fR
.IP \fIclock_t\fR 20
\fItms_utime\fP	
.IP \fIclock_t\fR 20
\fItms_stime\fP
.IP \fIclock_t\fR 20
\fItms_cutime\fP
.IP \fIclock_t\fR 20
\fItms_cstime\fP
.RE
.IP
.PP
The times of a terminated child process are included in the
.I tms_cutime 
and
.I tms_cstime
members of the parent when a 
.I wait
or 
.I waitpid 
function returns the process ID of this terminated child.  If a child
process has not waited for its terminated children, their times are 
not included in its times.
.PP
The value 
.I tms_utime 
is the CPU time charged for the execution of user
intructions.
.PP
The value
.I tms_stime
is the CPU time charged for execution by the system on behalf
of the process.
.PP
The value
.I tms_cutime
is the sum of the 
.IR tms_utime 's
s and
.IR tms_cutime 's
of the child processes.
.PP
The value 
.I tms_cstime 
is the sum of the 
.I tms_stime
s and
.I tms_cstime
s of the child processes.
.SH "RETURN VALUE"
Upon successful completion,
.I times
shall return the elapsed real time, in
clock ticks.  If 
.I times
fails, a value of ((
.I clock_t
)-1) is returned. 
.SH "SEE ALSO"
time(1), exec(2), fork(2), sysconf(2P), getrusage(2), wait(2), time(3C)
